The "Aggregate" header is ambiguous. I'm guessing it means one overall score of how far this row is from the reference row 12. The columns before it (AVERAGE, MIN, MAX, MEDIAN) all work on `$B$12:$W$12-$B4:$W4`, so this formula uses the same range.

A plain sum of those differences would be close to zero, because each row's values add up to about 1. Summing the absolute differences gives a more useful total distance. If "Aggregate" was meant to be Excel's `AGGREGATE` function instead, this guess would be wrong.

{"formula": "=SUM(ABS($B$12:$W$12-$B4:$W4))"}

K6 is "Forward Contract Stop Distance", so it most likely holds the gap between the entry price (D6) and the stop price (E6). L6 divides by it to get risk per point, so it needs to be a positive number. Wrapping the difference in ABS keeps it positive whether the trade is long or short.

The forward-contract price adjustment (H6−I6) shifts entry and stop by the same amount, so the distance between them stays the same. Using D6 and E6 directly therefore gives the right result.

{"formula": "=ABS(D6-E6)"}

K4 sits under the "Created" header of Table 2, so the formula should pull the Created timestamp from Table 1. It finds the row where Group, Code and Criterion all match the values in H4, I4 and J4.

Some combinations match more than one row in Table 1. For example, 123459 / DD / true matches rows 20 and 23. MAXIFS handles this by returning the latest Created timestamp among the matches. Whether you want the latest one is my assumption, since your intent isn't stated.

{"formula": "=MAXIFS($D$4:$D$39,$A$4:$A$39,H4,$B$4:$B$39,I4,$C$4:$C$39,J4)"}

The label in I8 asks for the new-item run rate over the last 14 days. This formula adds up the "New" counts in row 3 for the days from today − 13 through today, then divides by 14. Row 1 holds the day numbers, and A6 holds today's day of the month.

I used `SUMIFS` because it still works early in the month, when fewer than 14 days have passed. In that case it divides the available days by 14.

{"formula": "=SUMIFS(B3:AF3,B1:AF1,\">\"&A6-14,B1:AF1,\"<=\"&A6)/14"}

T7 sits under the "Avg. Positions held" header, and G7 holds the year (2020). The likeliest intent is to average the monthly position counts in AC for the months in AB that fall within that year.

A simple (J7+P7)/2 would not work here. J7 returns "-" because there is no 2019 data, so that version would error.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIFS(AC$7:AC$29,AB$7:AB$29,\">=\"&DATE(G7,1,1),AB$7:AB$29,\"<=\"&DATE(G7,12,31))"}

The cell right next to F17 (G17) already holds a formula that counts the longest run of consecutive "Matching Numbers" in C5:C13 that also appear in Data set 1 (F5:H13). F17 is in the same row and block, so you're most likely entering that same calculation.

Checking it against the data: 33, 57, 71 and 83 all appear in F5:H13 one after another. 0, 22, 101, 133 and 210 do not. That gives a run of 4, which matches the value shown in H17.

{"formula": "=MAX(FREQUENCY(C5:C13,IF(COUNTIF(F5:H13,C5:C13)=0,C5:C13)))-1"}

Cell Q3 holds "A", which matches the column header in B2 of Table 1. R3 is labelled "No. of Item", so the formula counts how many items have an "M" in that column.

`INDEX`/`MATCH` looks up which column to use from the value in Q3. This means the formula still works if Q3 is changed to B, C, D or E.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF(INDEX($B$3:$F$20,0,MATCH(Q3,$B$2:$F$2,0)),\"M\")"}

Column J ("month payed") should show which pay month each completed date in column I falls into. The pay periods start on the dates in B3:B41, which are sorted ascending. `LOOKUP` finds the last start date that is on or before the completed date and returns the matching month from column A.

For example, I3 (2011-11-11) falls after B3 (2011-10-23), so J3 returns 2011-11-01. If column I is blank, the formula returns #N/A.

{"formula": "=LOOKUP(I3,$B$3:$B$41,$A$3:$A$41)"}

This fills in "Total Vacation Days (since start date)" in H6. It copies the sick-hours formula in Q6: count the whole months since the start date, then multiply by a monthly accrual rate.

The 1.25 days per month (15 days a year) is a guess. The sheet doesn't show the vacation accrual rate, so check it against your actual policy. I used G3 (the start date, 2016-07-05) instead of the typed-in date that Q6 uses.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(G3,TODAY(),\"M\")*(1.25)"}

The "First Join" value in C2 (`11/20/23, 2:02:13 PM`) looks like a text export, since Teams attendance reports use a comma between date and time. This formula takes the part before the comma and converts it to a date. The pivot table counts unique days per employee, which fits a "Date Value" column built this way.

If C2 is actually a real date-time value rather than text, `=INT(C2)` would be the simpler choice.

{"formula": "=DATEVALUE(LEFT(C2,FIND(\",\",C2)-1))"}

The year isn't given, so the date will default to the current year.

{"formula": "=DATEVALUE(TEXTBEFORE(TEXTAFTER(A2,\" \"),\" \",2))"}

This takes the text after the weekday ("14 Mar 7:50pm"), keeps the part before the second space ("14 Mar"), and converts it to a date. B2 and C2 can then read it to show the day and month names.

Column B stores the completed dates as text, and each value starts with a weekday ("Friday, ..."). Excel can't convert text in that form to a date directly, so the formula cuts off everything up to the first comma. It then turns what's left into a date serial with `--`. That remainder is something like "April 09, 2010 5:34:54 PM".

For rows that aren't dates, such as "No need to take any action" or an empty cell, `IFERROR` returns a blank instead of an error. The conversion assumes US-style date settings.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(--MID(B2,FIND(\",\",B2)+2,LEN(B2)),\"\")"}

W6 sits under the "No. of Days" header for the October block. The nearest similar cell is Z6, which counts the days in its month with `=DAY(EOMONTH(Y6,0))`. Here V6 holds 2023-10-01, so the same pattern gives `=DAY(EOMONTH(V6,0))`, which returns 31.

The older blocks (K6, N6, Q6, T6) just repeat the start date instead of a day count. I followed the newer Z6 style because it is the most recent one and matches the "No. of Days" header.

{"formula": "=DAY(EOMONTH(V6,0))"}

R2 is labeled "Last Interest Payment Date". P2 adds one coupon period to it, and Q2 compares that result with COUPNCD in L2. So R2 most likely rebuilds the last coupon date independently as a cross-check.

The formula counts the coupons left with COUPNUM. It then steps back from maturity (F2) by that many periods, using 12/G2 months per period.

{"formula": "=EDATE(F2,-COUPNUM(D2,F2,G2,1)*12/G2)"}

The "Discount Rate" row most likely just pulls the YTM input from C10, so I've predicted `=$C$10`. This is a best guess from the row label.

The `$` signs are there because the PV formulas in row 16 drift when filled right. J16 points to D10 and K16 points to E10, which are both empty, so those discount factors come out as 1. An absolute reference to C10 here would let row 16 use `EXP(-I14*I13)` and fill across correctly.

{"formula": "=$C$10"}

Column D is titled "Time Rounded", and most readings fall just before the hour (08:59, 09:59). So I'm assuming you want the time in C2 rounded to the nearest hour.

One thing to check: readings like 23:59:58 round up to 24:00. Column E would then mark them "DAY" instead of "NIGHT". If you'd rather round down to the hour, use `=FLOOR(C2,"1:00")` instead.

{"formula": "=MROUND(C2,\"1:00\")"}

The header in E1 says "Using Forecast by including all values", so E3 should forecast from every known data point at once. The known values sit in G29:G35 (results) and H29:H35 (inputs).

I avoided pointing at column B directly because many B cells hold FORECAST formulas themselves. That could create circular references or mix estimated values in with real ones.

{"formula": "=FORECAST(C3,$G$29:$G$35,$H$29:$H$35)"}

I'm assuming E12 is the total price per m2 for a buyer paying in 6 instalments, set so its present value equals the cash price in D12. This also assumes the 5% discount rate in C6 is annual, so it is divided by 12 to get a monthly rate.

The formula works out the monthly payment over the 6 periods in C7, then multiplies by 6 to give the total instalment price. These assumptions aren't confirmed by the sheet, so check them before filling the column down.

{"formula": "=PMT($C$6/12,$C$7,-D12)*$C$7"}

The pay plan appears to set a per-show rate by tier. Row 2 holds each tier's lower bound (100, 111, …, 160) and row 4 holds the matching rate. `LOOKUP` finds the largest lower bound that is less than or equal to the show count in F11 and returns that tier's rate. Multiplying by F11 gives the Total Show $. For example, 112 shows falls in the 111–120 tier, so it pays 20 per show.

A count below 100 would return #N/A. If that matters, wrap the formula in `IFERROR`.

{"formula": "=F11*LOOKUP(F11,$C$2:$I$2,$C$4:$I$4)"}

Column L is headed "Punnam", one of the forum contributors whose formulas are compared in this row. Punnam's actual formula isn't shown anywhere in the sheet, so this is a best guess at a common forum answer.

It calculates hours worked from Start (E8) and End (F8). When the end time is at or before the start time, it adds one day, so overnight shifts work. This also makes a 12:00 AM–12:00 AM shift count as 24 hours.

{"formula": "=(F8-E8+(F8<=E8))*24"}

The label "Duplicate % Recovery (<20%)" points to the relative percent difference between the South average (K11) and the South Duplicate average (K15). That is the absolute difference divided by their mean.

I left out `*100` on the assumption that K19 is formatted as a percentage, which fits the "<20%" label; add `*100` if the cell is not percent-formatted. `IFERROR` returns a blank if either average is missing or not numeric.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(ABS(K11-K15)/AVERAGE(K11,K15),\"\")"}

I think L6 holds the Channel Width (labelled in K6), likely the `ChanWidth` value used in column J. This is a guess based on the sheet's layout, not a confirmed intent.

The formulas in columns O–R calculate a width as thickness × the Solid Board factor (L12) + Nib Width (L13), then round to one decimal. Applying the same pattern to the Board Thickness in I3 gives:

{"formula": "=ROUND(I3*L12+L13,1)"}

The values in column B look cumulative, since column C divides each row by B13 as the total. So I4 most likely needs last year's figure at the time in I2 (12:30), to compare against this year's 95 in I3. Because 12:30 isn't listed in column A, an approximate-match `VLOOKUP` returns the value for the latest time at or before 12:30, which is 12:00 → 90.

If you instead want a value between the 12:00 and 13:00 rows, linear interpolation would give 100. That would need a longer formula, such as `FORECAST` with `OFFSET`/`MATCH`.

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(I2,A2:B13,2,TRUE)"}

The payback year is the first year where cumulative profit minus investment (row 4) is zero or positive. This formula finds that column and returns its year number from row 5:

{"formula": "=INDEX(D5:M5,MATCH(TRUE,D4:M4>=0,0))"}

With the current data, row 4 turns positive in column J, so the result is **7** years. In older Excel versions without dynamic arrays, confirm the formula with Ctrl+Shift+Enter.

The IRR needs a cash flow series that starts with the initial investment as a negative value. Row 29 is set up that way: A29 is `=-B10` (the equity outflow), and B29 onward pull the aftertax cash flows from row 7.

I extended the range to column U to match the existing NPV formula in B27, which uses `B7:U7`. The visible context only goes to column T, so if the model actually ends there, `A29:T29` gives the same result.

{"formula": "=IRR(A29:U29)"}

M7 sits under "Longest Warnings", so it should mirror the "Shortest Warnings" formula in I7. The only changes are swapping SMALL for LARGE and using the rank in L7. I kept the same date condition as I7 for consistency.

{"formula": "=LARGE(IF(($B$7:$B$29>=$C$3)+($B$7:$B$29<=$E$3),$C$7:$C$29),L7)"}

Note that the existing formulas use `+` between the two date tests, which means OR. That condition is true for every date, so the Begin/End filter doesn't actually exclude anything. To filter properly, replace `+` with `*` here and in the I and M formulas.

Column D lists each KP in 1 m steps. Column B holds the rounded KP for every speed reading in column A. So E3 most likely averages all speeds in column A whose rounded KP in column B matches the KP in D3. The column references are locked with `$` so the formula can be filled down.

One caution: some D values carry floating-point noise (e.g. 70.5170000000001), so they may not exactly equal the B values and could return `#DIV/0!`. If that happens, use `ROUND(D3,3)` in place of `D3`.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIF($B:$B,D3,$A:$A)"}

The worksheet has two parallel blocks. Row 6 (labelled `[Person_1] (y=mx+b)`) holds `=LINEST(B4:K4,B3:K3^{1;2;3;4})` for the first block. Row 14 has the same label in the "Blank Cells Example" block, so the likely intent is the same formula pointed at that block's rows: row 12 as the y-values and row 11 as the x-values.

One caveat: row 11 has blank cells (C11, E11, F11, K11). `LINEST` may return an error rather than ignore those blanks. If so, you'd need to filter out the empty columns first.

{"formula": "=LINEST(B12:K12,B11:K11^{1;2;3;4})"}

Column J holds the shortage amounts, and rows 6–14 are a bracket table. Column A is the lower bound, C is the fixed deduction and D is the rate on the excess. So the deduction is the fixed amount plus the rate times the amount above the bracket's lower bound. An approximate-match `VLOOKUP` on `$A$6:$D$14` finds the right bracket for each value in J.

One thing to check: column C shows a flat 200 in every bracket. If the fixed deduction should build up from bracket to bracket (as the "Verify" values in column H hint), the results will need adjusting.

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(J6,$A$6:$D$14,3,TRUE)+(J6-VLOOKUP(J6,$A$6:$D$14,1,TRUE))*VLOOKUP(J6,$A$6:$D$14,4,TRUE)"}

The intent here is unclear, so this is a best guess rather than a confident prediction. B7 sits next to the "Rate Card" label, and F7, G7 and H7 all point to it, so it holds the retention rate. The only rate in the visible data is E7 (0.085), which matches the 8.5% hard-coded in E9, so I've referenced E7.

B7 might instead be meant to look up a rate from the turnover bands in column Q, but no rate column next to those bands is shown, so I didn't assume one.

{"formula": "=E7"}

The Match column (I9) next to Year (G) and CW (H) suggests you want the row position where both the Year and the Calendar Week match. This formula returns that position within the data range A3:B58.

The ranges are absolute, so you can fill it down to I11 for the "end" row. In older Excel versions you may need to confirm it with Ctrl+Shift+Enter.

{"formula": "=MATCH(1,($B$3:$B$58=G10)*($A$3:$A$58=H10),0)"}

Q16 sits under the "A" header, next to "Days (A:D)" in column N and "Months" in column P. So it most likely spreads fund A's 8,000 total shares (D3) across the months in proportion to each month's day count. The formula takes D3, multiplies by this month's days (N16) and divides by the total days in N16:N40.

This is a best guess. It does not exactly reproduce the existing values in column D (for example, 237.84 for 22 days). It also assumes column N will hold day counts, but N16:N40 is currently empty, so the formula returns an error until those are filled in.

{"formula": "=$D$3*N16/SUM(N$16:N$40)"}

The formula returns the highest flow in B19:B33 for low-flow days (category 1 in column C) whose run count in column F equals the longest continuous run, which J5 already computes.

One limitation: this only checks the flow on the last day of the longest run. It does not take the maximum across every day of that run. Doing that would need a much longer formula. This version follows the same `MAXIFS` pattern as J4 and J5.

{"formula": "=MAXIFS($B$19:$B$33,$C$19:$C$33,1,$F$19:$F$33,J5)"}

The allowance is 25% of the lower of cost and deemed cost. If deemed cost is 0, it is 25% of cost. The 25% rate comes from `$B$25`, and that reference is absolute so it stays fixed when the formula is copied across to D21:F21.

{"formula": "=IF(C20=0,C19,MIN(C19:C20))*$B$25"}

The Income Tax Value formulas in row 22 (`=MIN(C19:C20)-C21`) don't handle the zero case. In F22, `MIN` returns 0 instead of the cost. You could change it to `=IF(C20=0,C19,MIN(C19:C20))-C21` so it follows the same rule.

For each model, the latest patch common to all countries is the earliest of the per-country latest patches. Blank results (countries with no patch for that model) are ignored. Because patching is cumulative, that earliest date is the patch every country already has.

{"formula": "=MINIFS(M3:M8,M3:M8,\">0\")"}

This gives the expected results in F4:F5. For Model A, the per-country latest patches are 2024-12 (CAN, MEX) and 2025-01 (USA), so the formula returns 2024-12. For Model B, they are 2024-12, 2024-10, 2024-11 and 2024-11, so it returns 2024-10. The result is a date serial, so format the cell as a date (e.g., yyyy-mm) to display it like the expected results.

The cells in column O look like the spilled output of a single formula in O6. O7 (34) and O8 (16) are the full-year row totals for C7:N7 and C8:N8. O9 (76) is the grand total (26 + 34 + 16), and Pear's row adds to 26.

C9 already sums each column with `MMULT` and `SEQUENCE`. The matching row-wise version multiplies C6:N9 by a column of 1s. That returns each row's total, spilling down from O6 to O9.

{"formula": "=MMULT(--C6:N9,SEQUENCE(COLUMNS(C6:N9),1,1,0))"}

TAT here is the working days from Order Date (B2) to Completion Date (D2), with weekends excluded. `NETWORKDAYS` counts exactly that.

The vacation dates in column F are free text, not real dates, so they can't be used as the holidays argument. If TAT should run from Dictation Date instead, use `C2` in place of `B2`.

{"formula": "=NETWORKDAYS(B2,D2)"}

Q4 most likely holds the number of months left on the loan after a restructure point. In column Q, Q2 (`=P13`) takes the outstanding principal after payment 4, and Q3, Q5 and Q6 carry forward the original terms. So Q4 would be the original term (N4) minus the payment number in L13 (4), giving 12 − 4 = 8.

This is a best guess: the schedule in column L runs to 15 rows, so the user may intend a different remaining term.

{"formula": "=N4-L13"}

The current cell N3 sits right after the "NPV" label in M3. L3 holds 0.08, which looks like an annual discount rate, so N3 most likely calculates the NPV. Column K is headed "MTM" but is currently empty; it appears meant to hold monthly mark-to-market cash flows for rows 4–51.

Because the cash flows are monthly, I've divided the annual rate by 12. If the 8% is meant to be applied per period as-is, the formula would use `L3` instead of `L3/12`.

{"formula": "=NPV(L3/12,K4:K51)"}

The label in A36 is "PV of total free cash flows", and C36 holds the discount rate pulled from 'OAT Inputs'. So E36 most likely discounts the total free cash flows in E34:G34 at that rate with NPV.

The visible forecast runs only through column G. If the sheet has more forecast years, the range would need to extend past G34.

{"formula": "=NPV(C36,E34:G34)"}

J4 sits under the Year, Month and Percentile inputs, so it most likely returns the requested percentile of the values flagged in column E. Column E shows a day's value only when it matches the chosen year and month and is at least 1; otherwise it is blank text, which PERCENTILE ignores.

J3 holds 95, so it is divided by 100 to get the 0–1 fraction PERCENTILE expects. I used the whole column E:E because the data runs from 1980 to 2009 and the last row isn't shown.

{"formula": "=PERCENTILE(E:E,J3/100)"}

The "Total TWR" row should chain-link the monthly time-weighted returns in F6:F8. Each month's return is compounded as (1 + return), the results are multiplied together, and 1 is subtracted. This is the standard way to combine period returns into a total time-weighted return.

{"formula": "=PRODUCT(1+F6:F8)-1"}

In versions older than Excel 365, this formula must be confirmed with Ctrl+Shift+Enter. Otherwise, `=(1+F6)*(1+F7)*(1+F8)-1` gives the same result.

B11 is the bond's value, so the formula uses Excel's PRICE function. It takes the settlement date (B2), maturity date (B3), coupon rate (B5), required return (B6), frequency (B9) and basis (B10).

PRICE returns the price per $100 of face value, so the redemption argument is 100. The result is then scaled to the actual face value in B7 by multiplying by B7/100.

{"formula": "=PRICE(B2,B3,B5,B6,100,B9,B10)*B7/100"}

The amounts are negative, so "smallest" here means the most negative value. This formula takes the smallest amount whose Reason for Movement matches the code in C1 (2250). The `ROWS($D$2:D2)` part counts up as you copy down, giving 1st, 2nd and 3rd in D2:D4.

If "smallest return" instead means closest to zero, swap `SMALL` for `LARGE`. If your data goes past row 20, extend both ranges. In older Excel versions you may need to confirm it as an array formula with Ctrl+Shift+Enter.

{"formula": "=SMALL(IF($B$2:$B$20=$C$1,$A$2:$A$20),ROWS($D$2:D2))"}

D16 is labelled "sd" in E16. It should hold the standard deviation of the distribution in A11:C37. A11:A37 are the values, C11:C37 are their frequencies, and H2 is the total count.

The mean is already in D15, so the formula takes the square root of the frequency-weighted squared deviations from D15, divided by H2. It follows the same `SUMPRODUCT`/`H2` style as D15.

{"formula": "=SQRT(SUMPRODUCT((A11:A37-D15)^2,C11:C37)/H2)"}

P3 sits next to the fruit in O3 (Melons), under the QUANTITY header. P1 and Q1 hold the start and end dates. This formula adds up all quantities for that fruit across the date columns D:M that fall within that range. It only counts rows whose country is in the selected list T4:T6.

This assumes the D1:M1 headers are real dates. It also assumes T4:T6 is meant as a country filter, which I inferred from the layout. If it isn't, drop the `ISNUMBER(MATCH(...))` part. O3 is a relative reference, so the formula fills down to P4:P6.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(($C$2:$C$29=O3)*ISNUMBER(MATCH($B$2:$B$29,$T$4:$T$6,0))*($D$1:$M$1>=$P$1)*($D$1:$M$1<=$Q$1)*$D$2:$M$29)"}

The Output table looks like it converts each sales figure using the FX Table. The test in F9 (`=+E13*D4`) multiplies Jan sales for Region 1 AUD by the AUD Jan rate, which supports this.

The formula below multiplies the sales value by the matching rate. `INDEX/MATCH` looks up the rate using the row's currency (column K) and the month in the header (row 12). The mixed references (`$K13`, `L$12`) let you fill it across L:N and down to row 21.

{"formula": "=E13*INDEX($D$4:$G$6,MATCH($K13,$C$4:$C$6,0),MATCH(L$12,$D$3:$G$3,0))"}

D4 is Provider 2's "Hours Used Per Month" for October, so it should match the row 2 formula with the tracker references shifted down 11 rows. The "Remaining" links step by 11 rows (C9, C20, C31), so Provider 2's block likely starts at row 21 instead of row 10.

The suggestion below follows the cleaner E2–O2 version of the formula. It anchors the range at `$F$21` and uses `C21`, `C22` and `C23` (8, 8 and 4 hours). `COLUMN(A$1)` makes it pick the correct month when filled across.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF(OFFSET('PTO Tracker 2025'!$F$21,,(COLUMN(A$1)-1)*8,6,7),'PTO Tracker 2025'!$C21)*8+COUNTIF(OFFSET('PTO Tracker 2025'!$F$21,,(COLUMN(A$1)-1)*8,6,7),'PTO Tracker 2025'!$C22)*8+COUNTIF(OFFSET('PTO Tracker 2025'!$F$21,,(COLUMN(A$1)-1)*8,6,7),'PTO Tracker 2025'!$C23)*4"}

Row 34 is labelled "Other" and sits directly under the "car" and "COMMERCIAL" rows. The formulas in E32 and E33 count USED vehicles whose model and category match a pair in [1]Sheet2. This formula follows the same pattern for C34, so it counts USED vehicles that Sheet2 lists under "Other".

If "Other" is meant to be everything left over, `=COUNTIF($B$4:$B$31,"USED")-E32-E33` would fit instead. I went with the copied pattern because Sheet2 may have its own "Other" entries.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(--($B$4:$B$31=\"USED\"),--(ISNUMBER(MATCH($D$4:$D$31&C34,[1]Sheet2!$A$1:$A$100&[1]Sheet2!$B$1:$B$100,0))))"}

I read H9 as the "n" column: how many column B values fall in the a-range from F9 to G9.

The formula counts values from F9 (included) up to G9 (not included). The ranges share boundaries (0–1, 1–2, 2–3), so the last bin (row 11) needs `"<="&G11` instead. Otherwise values equal to the max (3) won't be counted when this is filled down.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS(B:B,\">=\"&F9,B:B,\"<\"&G9)"}

Cell E6 sits next to the "First Year" label, so I'm reading it as the share of the first year's budget that falls inside the period. That is a guess. E6 could instead be meant to hold just the start year (`=YEAR(C2)`).

The formula finds the budget for the start-date year in the Year/Budget table (G2:H37). It then multiplies that budget by the fraction of the year left from the start date to the start of the next year. For a start date of 2015-07-01, that fraction is 0.5, giving 50.

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(YEAR(C2),$G$2:$H$37,2,FALSE)*YEARFRAC(C2,DATE(YEAR(C2)+1,1,1))"}

Cell D25 is the "Delivery Percentage%" row, and E25 holds the same calculation for column E. This completion copies E25's formula with the references switched to `$D$7:$D$18`. It returns blank when the column is empty or the division errors. Otherwise it gives the share of "Del" entries out of all non-"N/A" cells.

E25's formula appears to have a flaw: the result is only calculated when the "Del" count is 0, and otherwise it returns FALSE. This formula reproduces that behaviour as-is to stay consistent with the neighbouring cell.

{"formula": "=IF(OR(COUNTA($D$7:$D$18)=0,ISERROR(COUNTIF($D$7:$D$18,\"Del\")/(COUNTIF($D$7:$D$18,\"<>N/A\")))),\"\",IF(COUNTIF($D$7:$D$18,\"Del\")=0,COUNTIF($D$7:$D$18,\"Del\")/(COUNTIF($D$7:$D$18,\"<>N/A\"))))"}

The intent isn't clear here. Column C has no formulas or values in any row, not even in C1, so there's no pattern to extend. You've only typed `=`, which doesn't hint at what C2 should calculate.

